A||||4||JohnA||2 Questions 1988 Formula Red/Grey||||5||
Z||000000||JohnA||08-07-2001||09:36 AM||||\image{1988 formula}<BR>First: My tempeture gauge has a mind of its own. Up/Down sometime nothing, trouble is probably the sending unit, where is it??<BR>Second: I don't seem to have a damper on my steering assembly or a place to bolt one, was this normal for a 1988?<BR>Look forward to any answers, Thanks, JOHN||||reg||5
Z||000001||DRH||08-07-2001||10:06 AM||||1. It could be a loose connection but the guage is known for having the needle get loose on the shaft. (Probably from slamming into the stop every time you start it). Does the needle peg every time you turn the key to start?<P>2. 88 did not have a damper. Rodney Dickman sells an add on unit. <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rodneydickman.com</A>   I don't know of anyone personally that has used it though.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnA||08-08-2001||09:15 AM||||Thanks DRH, I would have preferred that it be a loose wire or bad sensor on the engine. The gauge must be more complicated to change. Do you know where on the engine sensor is??<BR>Do you think the damper would help at 80 mph. That is when the frontend start to shake some, not that I drive around at that speed, but I have. A garage balanced the tires, maybe I should have them checked again. <BR>Thanks for your interest in my problem, JOHN ||||reg||12
Z||000003||DRH||08-08-2001||10:10 AM||||Does your guage peg out when you turn the key to start? Does it go to 0 when everything is off?<P>The sender is located on the rear head right next to the coil on the V6. It will have a green/yellow and a green wire running to it. The green/yellow is for the guage. Unplug and jumper it to a good ground with the ignition switch on and the guage should go all the way hot. (Grounding the green wire should light the coolant light)<P>My understanding was that the steering damper was to reduce feedback from bumps, not to correct shaking problems. Some places don't have the equipment to balance well enough for high speeds. It could also be a slightly out of round tire. Also, push/pull on the front wheels to see if there is any play in the rack/tie rods.<P>Oh, and welcome to the Forum! ||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||08-08-2001||12:34 PM||||theres a little brass or copper finger in the gauge itself. they usually corrode a little causing gauges to act erraticly. just pull the gauge and clean it with some sandpaper. the little piece is what makes the gauge ground to chassis.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 08-08-2001).]||||reg||1
